<section class="sec" id="why">
  <div class="wrap problem-grid">
    <div class="reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">The problem</span>
      <p class="quote">
        Most agent logs are written by <span class="flag">the same system</span> whose
        behavior they describe.
      </p>
      <p class="problem-body">
        If the agent, runtime, or operator is compromised, those logs can be
        incomplete or false. So Sello flips who holds the pen.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="reveal invert">
      <span class="lbl">The architectural inversion</span>
      <div class="row">
        <span class="arr">before</span><span
          >agent writes <b>its own log</b></span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="row">
        <span class="arr">after</span><span
          >service signs <b class="accent">the receipt it observed</b></span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="spacer"></div>
      <div class="foot">
        The service was present for the action, but sits outside the agent's
        logging path. It signs what it saw, and nothing it didn't.
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
